To determine how many shelves Tom would fill up, we need to figure out how many diet sodas fit on one shelf and how many shelves are needed to hold all the diet sodas.

One shelf can hold 7 diet sodas, so the number of shelves required will be:

\boxed{\text{Shelves needed} = \dfrac{\text{Total number of diet sodas}}{\text{Number of diet sodas per shelf}}}

\boxed{\text{Shelves needed} = \dfrac{52}{7}}

Similarly,
\boxed{\text{Shelves needed} = 7.43 \approx 7 (rounded up)}

So, Tom would need 8 shelves to hold all his diet sodas.

